# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Pitch Crosses, Kick Fires

**Story & Context:** The opening track teaches the central rule immediately. Mai plucks a high natural harmonic and begins a slow downward bend. Darius remains almost silent until the pitch crosses the first predetermined threshold, at which point a five-kick footwork burst fires across less than one bar. As the bend continues through its unstable midpoint, both snare and sub disappear completely. Linh waits until the pitch settles, then enters with a short bass-trumpet smear that begins below the destination and rises into it.

Jae maintains a three-hit woodblock figure through the drum cut, allowing the fast internal pulse to remain perceptible. On the second major bend, Darius reverses the rule: crossing the threshold removes kicks instead of adding them. By the final section, the listener no longer knows whether a pitch crossing will create impact or silence.

The track ends with Mai completing one final bend after every electronic channel has been muted. Only the flexible rod settling back to rest and one dry woodblock strike remain.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close documentary image of a đàn bầu pitch rod bending beside three horizontal tape marks, sampler display showing a clustered kick pattern, bass-trumpet bell and wooden block in shallow focus. No full lyrics; sparse spoken cues limited to “cross the line,” “kick fires,” “hold the bend,” and “land before the bass.”

## Track 2: The Snare Vanishes Mid-Glide

**Story & Context:** The second track starts with the most recognizable footwork pattern on the EP: clipped snares and syncopated kicks at 160 BPM. Mai plays short harmonics at first, keeping pitch movement narrow. Every fourth phrase she begins a much wider upward glide. The moment the pitch leaves its stable lower region, Darius removes the snare entirely while leaving kick fragments and woodblocks intact.

Linh answers not with notes but breath and valve noise during the first two glide cycles. Only on the third does a low bass-trumpet tone enter, timed to the precise moment Mai’s pitch stabilizes. Jae uses contact microphones on a lighting rail to replace one missing snare with a hard metallic click whose decay is much longer than the drum sample it replaces.

Halfway through, the snare returns during a bend for the first time, but every kick disappears instead. The groove feels wrong even though the tempo never changes. The final eight bars contain full drums but no đàn bầu; the listener still anticipates bends because the arrangement has taught the ear to associate rhythmic instability with pitch motion.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Empty snare lane on a drum-machine grid projected onto black soundstage curtains, đàn bầu string caught mid-vibration, metal lighting rail with contact microphone taped to it, red record light glowing overhead. Spoken fragments limited to “snare goes missing,” “keep the grid,” “wait for the landing,” and “now switch the absence.”

## Track 3: Rod Returns After the Last Burst

**Story & Context:** The EP closer begins with Darius using the densest kick choreography on the record while Mai plays only isolated harmonics without bending them. The absence of pitch movement makes the drum density feel strangely static. Linh contributes compact bass-trumpet fifths, and Jae uses woodblocks almost like an acoustic hi-hat.

After thirty-two bars, Mai begins the longest bend on the EP, rising slowly through several harmonic regions. Instead of increasing energy, Darius removes elements one by one: first hats, then snare, then sub, then all kicks. The faster instrument produces the longer silence. Linh follows the bend with a half-valve smear but stops before the destination, refusing to complete it for her.

For sixteen bars, only đàn bầu, woodblock, and soundstage room tone remain. When Mai finally settles the pitch and returns the flexible rod toward neutral, Darius allows one final three-kick burst. No full beat follows it. Jae strikes the lighting rail once, and the metal decay carries into silence.

